      "id": 1,
      "text": "Livermore was founded in 1869 as a railroad town named after Robert Livermore, a rancher who settled there in the 1840s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livermore%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Carnegie Library, built in 1892, now houses the Livermore Heritage Guild’s History Center and Museum, preserving the city’s early history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.achp.gov/preserve-america/community/livermore-california"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory, established in 1952, gave the city its name a place in the periodic table with the element livermorium.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livermore%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Livermore’s first public cemetery, Oak Knoll, was abandoned after natural disasters and now serves as a sports field at Granada High School.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Livermore%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The city is part of California’s oldest wine region; pioneers like Wente and Concannon planted grapes in the 1880s, founding iconic wineries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
